1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. A Paris astronomer saw — a star
map made only of lines.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
The lines that join the stars in your
app: who drew them?

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Not the IAU. In the 1920s it fixed 88
constellations, but only their borders,
like a map. Never the lines.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
So one lion can be drawn many ways. The
star app Stellarium, by default: 10
lines.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
H. A. Rey, who drew Curious George: 18
lines.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:36,000
And in 1786, one man drew 32: Alexandre
Ruelle, a “student astronomer” at the
Paris Observatory.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Star maps then hid the stars inside
mythical figures and animals. Ruelle
took the pictures out.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Sickle, body, hind legs: all cut into
triangles and polygons. Even five stars
of today’s Leo Minor are in it.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Sadly, it came too early and did not
catch on. European star maps took up
lines slowly, in the 19th century.

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The lines in your app: centuries of
people’s choices, stacked up.

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Leo rides high in the south. The bright
orange dot by its Sickle is Mars: don’t
connect it.

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Take a sheet of paper and join the stars
your way. Your lines are a sky culture
too.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Australia: an emu made of darkness.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

